; 29a-intervals-and-progressions.kurt
;
; The numbers between two numbers
;
; interval.kurt names the intervals the way Mathlib does: `Icc a b` is closed (`a ≤ x ≤ b`), `Ico a b` and `Ioc a b` are half
; open (`a ≤ x < b`, `a < x ≤ b`), `Ioo a b` is open (`a < x < b`). Each is a set of numbers, and
; membership is its definition ("in-Icc", ...). Brackets `[a, b]` are not used: matrix.kurt has them.

load natural, interval
calc on

; 1. A number is in the closed interval when it is between its ends -- the ends included.

show 1 ∈ Icc 1 5 ∧ 3 ∈ Icc 1 5 ∧ 5 ∈ Icc 1 5
proof
    1 <= 1 ∧ 1 <= 5
    1 ∈ Icc 1 5                  ; in-Icc
    1 <= 3 ∧ 3 <= 5
    3 ∈ Icc 1 5
    1 <= 5 ∧ 5 <= 5
    5 ∈ Icc 1 5
qed

; 2. The open interval leaves the ends out: `1` is not in `Ioo 1 5`, it would have to be below itself.

show ¬(1 ∈ Ioo 1 5)
proof
    assume 1 ∈ Ioo 1 5
        1 < 1 ∧ 1 < 5            ; in-Ioo
        1 < 1
        ¬(1 < 1)
        1 < 1 ∧ ¬(1 < 1)
        ⊥
    ¬(1 ∈ Ioo 1 5)
qed

; 3. A set of numbers can also be written with `...`: `{a, ..., c}` are the numbers from `a` to `c` in steps of one
; (the natural numbers `k` with `a + k ≤ c`), `{a, b, ...}` in steps of `b - a`:

show 3 ∈ {1, ..., 5}
proof
    3 = 1 + 2
    2 ∈ Nat
    3 <= 5
    ∃ k ∈ Nat (3 = 1 + k ∧ 3 <= 5)
    3 ∈ {1, ..., 5}              ; range
qed
